   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "A practice is a “way of doing” some activity, involving how people in a culture not only think, but also behave, speak, feel, and interact with objects in the environment. Consider, for instance, a way of cooking in a given culture. Cooking involves bodily movements that individuals reproduce, objects in the environment that individuals handle in routine ways, explicit and background knowledge, and people’s intentions and choices. Individuals, according to practice theory, are always involved in",
   "md_text": "A practice is a “way of doing” some activity, involving how people in a culture not only think, but also behave, speak, feel, and interact with objects in the environment. Consider, for instance, a way of cooking in a given culture. Cooking involves bodily movements that individuals reproduce, objects in the environment that individuals handle in routine ways, explicit and background knowledge, and people’s intentions and choices. Individuals, according to practice theory, are always involved in"
  },
  {
   "i": 131,
   "shape_h": "ol",
   "shape_m": "ol",
   "html_text": "A crude mentalistic theory: the fact that the dance is a foxtrot is a product of the fact that I believe it is a foxtrot. A crude naturalistic theory: social categories are natural features of reality, with the physical structure of the world breaking reality down into categories, one of which is the foxtrot. A crude linguistic theory: we define the word ‘foxtrot’, and that definition specifies the conditions for an entity to be a foxtrot.",
   "md_text": "A crude mentalistic theory: the fact that the dance is a foxtrot is a product of the fact that I believe it is a foxtrot. A crude naturalistic theory: social categories are natural features of reality, with the physical structure of the world breaking reality down into categories, one of which is the foxtrot. A crude linguistic theory: we define the word ‘foxtrot’, and that definition specifies the conditions for an entity to be a foxtrot."
  }
